this proposal proposes amending um portions of the unified development code in respect to accessory dwelling units or ADUs um just for the information of the commission or for any members of the public who may not be aware of what an ADU is an accessory dwelling unit is a second unit on the same property as a principal dwelling unit that can take many forms um sometimes it's an detached unit all by itself um sometimes it's attached to the house sometimes it's in a garage sometimes it's in the principal building above a garage or in the principal building in a basement um just there's a lot of different ways um this could be done but the essential fact of the matter is it's two dwelling units on one property um

our current ADU code requirements um they exist in the ad in the um unified development code right now um ADUs are allowed as an administrative use in all of our normal residential zones um and a they are limited to only one per lot um either and the property owner must live in either the principal building or the ADU um the size is limited to 40% of the principal building um or if it's in the basement it can be the same size as the principal dwelling unit um they are required to generally maintain the neighborhood character and they are required to provide one additional off- streetet parking space um the proposed amendment would strike the requirement for a property owner to live on the property and

it would strike that the additional parking space must be off off streetet um our unified development code has a lot when it comes to on street parking um uh allowances so a a commercial business or an apartment complex can meet some of their parking requirements by providing parking on the street um or credit some of their parking requirements by parking on the street um this would just allow for some more flexibility when it comes to accessory dwelling units um also um the phraseology in the code right now says it has to be on a discrete portion of the lot but by doing this you could have a third stall on your driveway for example it wouldn't have to be behind the house or hidden or something like that so

it just gives some more flexibility for the parking also when it comes to accessory dwelling unit owner occupancy um the national trends um discourage that as a poison pill toward accessory dwelling units um as it were um it limits the number and um ability to do accessory dwelling units and at the end of the day um the population the traffic the noise the differences between that between an owner occupied ADU and a notowner occupied ADU are fairly minimal um with this code amendment um there would be some conforming amendments to our code um right now our code does call a flat overflat duplex differently than an ADU um however once we have an ADU not being required to be owner occupant occupied the difference between a flat or flat duplex and

an ADU is minimal to nothing really um I did talk to building departments in Montana where they are required to have ADUs without and they're required to not require the state does not allow municipalities to require own occupancy for those ADUs so I talked to cities of similar size to Cheyenne in Montana and their building department treats an ADU the same as they would a flat or a flat duplex there's no no differences um whereas our current building official has has given some more lenuency in building code um considering kind of a single family home as long as the owner is there um that would be taken away so that is kind of a little bit of a trade-off here is that by removing the owner occupancy now all

ADUs are duplexes but also by doing that um staff proposes to um put all flat over flat duplexes into uh the ADU section because there is some ambiguity in our code as to how to treat flat or flat duplexes um our duplex lot type in the UDC really only considers sideby-side duplexes and therefore does frontage and used to do lot area and does setbacks and all these things kind of based off a sidebyside model doesn't give staff good direction as to what to do with a flat over flat model by making all single uh sorry semi- detached dwellings side by side and then making all flat or flats uh accessory dwellings that gives staff clarity into it as to enforcement and

as to what regulations apply to which type of unit um we have received uh multiple letters um supporting this text amendment um one of which I believe made it into your packet ahead of time others have come in since that time i do have I don't know what ones you've received so I have printed out a couple of copies um one from the Affordable Housing Task Force one from um the uh Southwest Wyoming Builders Association Southeast sorry Southeast Wyoming Builders Association and um another from I I the Larmy County Community Partnership or LCCP so I have multiple letters um of support hopefully you've seen them either in your email or with the staff report most of them come with similar recommendation similar commentary um

so it it's if you've seen one you've probably seen something similar to the other three not exactly the same there are differences between them but generally all of them have expressed support um staff did review our ADU applications that we've received and we couldn't find any pattern as far as like a builder who specializes in ADUs who may be upset about the potential change to codes um so we didn't find anybody to notify with that potential change and if that was going to be a concern um essentially most builders say "Hey owner you want to do this this is what you're going to have to do in order to have an ADU on your property." Um

